CASE = [
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> Ramesh, a farmer near Nagpur in Maharashtra, grows cotton on black soil. The soil is black in colour, becomes sticky when wet and develops wide cracks in the dry season. It was formed by the weathering of the volcanic rocks of the Deccan trap region and is rich in calcium carbonate, magnesium, potash and lime but poor in phosphoric content. "
   r"(i) By what other name is this soil known? (ii) From which rocks is it formed? (iii) Name one crop other than cotton suited to this soil. (iv) Why is this soil called 'self-ploughing'?",
   r"(i) Regur soil (1). (ii) Volcanic rocks (lava) (1). (iii) Any one of wheat, sugarcane, soyabean, jowar, linseed, citrus fruits or vegetables (1). (iv) In the dry season it develops deep cracks, which allow air to enter and loosen and aerate the soil, as if it were ploughed itself (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> In a village in Rajasthan, the panchayat owns a pasture and a village pond used by all families, while each family also owns its own fields. The sea off the coast up to 12 nautical miles belongs to the nation, and the ocean beyond the Exclusive Economic Zone is administered by international institutions. "
   r"(i) What are resources owned collectively by a community called? (ii) Give one example from the passage. (iii) Up to how many nautical miles from the coast do the territorial waters extend? (iv) Who controls ocean resources beyond 200 nautical miles of the EEZ, and why can no country use them freely?",
   r"(i) Community-owned resources (1). (ii) The village pasture or pond (1). (iii) 12 nautical miles (1). (iv) International institutions control them; they belong to everyone, so any use needs international agreement (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q(r"<b>Case study.</b> A farmer in a hilly district of Uttarakhand finds that after heavy rain, running water has cut deep channels through his sloping field, making it unfit for cultivation. His neighbour has made steps on the slope and sowed crops in strips, with a row of trees at the field edge. "
   r"(i) Name the type of erosion described. (ii) What are such badly cut lands called? (iii) Name the method of making steps on slopes. (iv) Explain how strip cropping and shelter belts help prevent soil erosion.",
   r"(i) Gully erosion (1). (ii) Bad land (in Chambal basin, ravines) (1). (iii) Terrace farming (1). (iv) In strip cropping, strips of grass are left between crops to break the force of wind and water; shelter belts of trees check wind and stabilise the soil (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

